{"parentUuid":"b0120b29-d614-4dea-87ab-bf72aab8ed05","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011ChNvc8BtYAmcM6M6QQs3v","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**The Keeper of Hollow Point**\n\nFor forty-one years, Ada Marsh climbed the one hundred and twelve iron steps of Hollow Point lighthouse every evening at dusk. She wound the clockwork that turned the lens, trimmed the wick, and polished the brass until her reflection stared back like a stranger.\n\nThe island had no harbor, only a jagged shelf of rock and one stubborn gull. Ships passed in the distance, their lights no bigger than matchheads, and not one ever turned toward her.\n\nIn her final winter, a letter arrived from the mainland. The light would be automated by spring. A machine would blink on its own, and no one would climb the stairs again.\n\nAda read it twice, folded it into her apron pocket, and climbed up to the lamp room as usual. At midnight the storm arrived, the kind that hurls the sea over the rocks and tests every hinge in the tower. The radio crackled. A fishing boat had lost its engine and was drifting toward the shelf.\n\nIts antenna had been torn away, so Ada could not answer by radio. She did what she had always done. She swung the lamp to full power, pulled the shutters aside, and let the beam sweep the water, slow and steady, hour after hour.\n\nNear dawn, a red flare trembled up from the water. Then another. The boat was turning, following the beam toward the gap in the rocks.\n\nIt slipped through at first gray light, its hull scraped but whole. Three men climbed onto the island, soaked and shaking. The youngest looked up at the tower and said, \"We could see you the whole way.\"\n\nAda only nodded and set the kettle on.\n\nLater, the youngest one asked why she had stayed so long on a rock with no harbor. Ada thought about the question while the water boiled over the old stove. \"Because someone out there is always counting on the light,\" she said, though she had never once been sure of it herself until that morning.\n\nThat spring, the automated light was installed as promised. It blinked faithfully, and it never once needed her. Ada took the ferry to the mainland with the letter still folded in her pocket. Every night for the rest of her life, she looked up at the sky and wondered whether the lamp still turned."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16652,"cache_read_input_tokens":27618,"output_tokens":4875,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":4169},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16652,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4875,"cache_read_input_tokens":27618,"cache_creation_input_tokens":16652,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":16652},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011ChNvc86gkthVxzdp31Ps3","requestRef":"ac9ee731-01c0-463e-bb7e-0391e3dbf9a4","requestedModel":"claude-haiku-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"ab686e35-d6d3-47e3-81db-66cef29b7cff","timestamp":"2026-11-25T03:20:07.498Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","madeBy":"model","session_id":"c6b773af-da92-49f8-a4dc-a5f304d5ba0c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/user/src/spike/plain","sessionId":"c6b773af-da92-49f8-a4dc-a5f304d5ba0c","version":"2.1.332","gitBranch":"HEAD","dataFlowOn":true}
